--TEST--
`Core\Test`'s failure messages render each scalar as itself -- `false` is the word, an `int` and a `uint` of the same digits agree, and a `float` and a `decimal` each keep their own spelling -- `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The container arms next door say a *size* because a message goes to a build
// log; the scalar arms have the opposite obligation, because a scalar is the
// leaf `assertEqualsDeep` has just finished naming and quoting it is the whole
// point. So each arm renders the value as itself, and none of them borrows a
// conversion the language already has: `bool as string` gives `false` the
// empty string, which would print a failure with a column missing.
//
// `assertSame` binds both arguments to one type variable, so every row below
// needs a differing partner of its own type -- which is also why `null` is
// reached through a `?int` rather than on its own.

int $refused = 0;

// `null` is one word, and the `?int` carrying it is the only way a nullable
// subject reaches this member at all.
?int $nothing = null;
?int $some = 5;
try { Core\Test::assertSame($nothing, $some); } catch (Throwable $nulled) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $nulled->message, "\n"; }

// The row `bool as string` cannot serve: it renders `false` as the empty
// string, so a message built out of it would read `$actual` is , which is the
// failure this arm exists to avoid. Both words are printed in full.
bool $no = false;
bool $yes = true;
try { Core\Test::assertSame($no, $yes); } catch (Throwable $flagged) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $flagged->message, "\n"; }
echo "bool as string gives [", $no as string, "] and [", $yes as string, "]\n";

// An `int` prints its sign, and a `uint` prints its own range rather than the
// two's-complement reading of it: `18446744073709551615` is not `-1`, and the
// line above proves this case can tell those apart.
int $big = 9223372036854775807;
int $neg = -1;
try { Core\Test::assertSame($big, $neg); } catch (Throwable $signed) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $signed->message, "\n"; }

uint $same = 9223372036854775807 as uint;
uint $past = 18446744073709551615;
try { Core\Test::assertSame($same, $past); } catch (Throwable $unsigned) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $unsigned->message, "\n"; }

// A `float` renders through Rust's own `to_string`, which is why `1.0` is `1`
// and not `1.0` -- and a `decimal` renders through its own, which keeps the
// scale it was written with, so `1.00` stays `1.00`. The two arms sit next to
// each other and answer differently for the same number on purpose.
float $one = 1.0;
float $half = 0.5;
try { Core\Test::assertSame($one, $half); } catch (Throwable $floated) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $floated->message, "\n"; }

decimal $padded = 1.00;
decimal $money = 19.90;
try { Core\Test::assertSame($padded, $money); } catch (Throwable $scaled) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $scaled->message, "\n"; }

// The invariance, asserted rather than read off the two lines above: the same
// digits under two different tags render the same string. A member that let
// the tag reach the rendering -- a suffix, a width, a sign it only prints for
// one of them -- fails here while both of its own lines still look right.
string $intMsg = "";
string $uintMsg = "";
try { Core\Test::assertSame($big, $neg); } catch (Throwable $keptInt) { $intMsg = $keptInt->message; }
try { Core\Test::assertSame($same, $past); } catch (Throwable $keptUint) { $uintMsg = $keptUint->message; }
string $intShown = Core\Str::before(Core\Str::after($intMsg, "is ") ?? "", ",") ?? "";
string $uintShown = Core\Str::before(Core\Str::after($uintMsg, "is ") ?? "", ",") ?? "";
int $agreed = 0;
try { Core\Test::assertSame($intShown, $uintShown); $agreed = $agreed + 1; } catch (Throwable $disagreed) { echo $disagreed->message, "\n"; }
echo "shown=", $intShown, "\n";

// Counted, not read off the lines: an arm that answered plausibly instead of
// refusing subtracts from one of these.
echo "refused=", $refused, "/6 agreed=", $agreed, "/1\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is null, `$expected` is 5
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is false, `$expected` is true
bool as string gives [] and [1]
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is 9223372036854775807, `$expected` is -1
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is 9223372036854775807, `$expected` is 18446744073709551615
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is 1, `$expected` is 0.5
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is 1.00, `$expected` is 19.90
shown=9223372036854775807
refused=6/6 agreed=1/1
